at this camp an hour outside of rome  more than a thousand afghan refugees are  starting a new life  now far from the country they called  home many of them like this man felt  they had no choice but to flee  i work with italian and afghan military  so i was a target for the taliban  he like others we spoke to asked us not  to show his face out of fear for loved  ones back in  afghanistan i am happy to be here but i  am extremely worried about my family  i haven't been able to contact them for  seven days i don't know how they're  doing or where they are  it's a story repeated here among those  who worked with italian or other  international forces during the war as  the taliban closed in they rushed to  kabul airport some lost family in the  chaotic rush to escape and haven't seen  them since  one refugee we met served as a policeman  in the former government he told us he  felt particularly at risk of taliban  retribution since august 14th nearly 5  000 afghan refugees have arrived in  italy the largest number of any european  union country i feel safe here  but i'm a bit worried about  the my future how can i organize  everything how how can i continue to  my higher education those running the  camp are trying to address such concerns  of course they want to know about their  future the process of inclusion in our  country which steps now they are going  to do in uh to be included in our  society about the  the schools for the children  the job  and all this of course we cannot give an  answer to all of these questions but to  engage explaining the process step by  step this camp run by the italian red  cross is the largest processing center  for the refugees in italy more than 1300  afghan evacuees have been sheltered here  temporarily as they await longer term  placements after being screened for  covet 19 many received vaccinations  after being processed here the evacuees  are then taken by bus to other shelters  and hotels across italy  officials hope to resettle everyone at  this camp by friday the first step in  helping these refugees rebuild their  lives in a new country adam rainey al  jazeera avezano italy
 

